.TH HOME-DECLUTTER 1 "2026-08-07" "senechal" "senechal manual"
.SH NAME
home-declutter.py \- heuristic $HOME decluttering, hardlink\-guarded and garde\-backed
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tools/home-declutter.py
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.B scan
.br
.B tools/home-declutter.py
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.B quarantine
.br
.B tools/home-declutter.py
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.B verify
.br
.B tools/home-declutter.py
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.B purge
[\fB\-\-confirm\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B home-declutter.py
finds and clears real clutter out of \fB$HOME\fR \- stray caches, old
installers already installed elsewhere, name\-shaped leftover debris \-
without ever deleting a live file directly.
.B quarantine
only ever moves a file (\fBmv\fR, never \fBrm\fR); the one irreversible
step,
.BR purge ,
is always a separate, explicit, manual action.
.PP
No candidate is quarantined on a guess. Each one must clear two
independent gates first:
.TP
.B the hardlink guard
Every file under the candidate must have \fIst_nlink\fR == 1 (belt).
Anything that trips this is independently re\-checked with
.BR find (1)
.B \-xdev \-samefile
over the configured search roots (suspenders), so the refusal names
the sibling path it is linked from instead of failing silently.
.TP
.B recoverability
Either the candidate matches a declared \(lqregenerable\(rq pattern
(caches, \fB__pycache__\fR, \fI*.tmp\fR \- expected to be recreated,
not restored), or
.BR garde (1)
confirms it has an off\-machine copy. Real content with no backup and
no regenerable classification is left alone and reported as such.
.SH CANDIDATE CLASSES
.TP
.B regenerable
Whole\-tree pattern match, roots configured narrow (e.g.
\fB~/Downloads\fR, \fB~/Desktop\fR). A matched entry \- file or
directory \- is quarantined as a unit and not descended into further.
Never needs \fBgarde\fR coverage: by definition it is expected to
regenerate, not be restored.
.TP
.B stale_download
Extension + age match, top\-level only within its roots (e.g. an
\fI.AppImage\fR or \fI.deb\fR older than \fIstale_days\fR that has
already been installed elsewhere). Real content, so it always needs
\fBgarde\fR coverage.
.TP
.B debris
Name\-shaped stray\-leftover signals \- pre\-migration backup suffixes,
conflict\-resolution snapshot suffixes, temp/tmp\-token names \- plus,
independent of name, zero\-byte files. Walks its roots recursively but
is files\-only: a directory is never itself quarantined whole, even if
its name matches, because that blast radius is bigger than this class
is meant for. Real content, so it always needs \fBgarde\fR coverage
like \fBstale_download\fR.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B scan
Find every candidate, run both gates, and print each one's verdict
(\fBSAFE\fR, \fBSKIP_HARDLINK\fR, \fBSKIP_UNCOVERED\fR, or
\fBSKIP_UNKNOWN\fR) with its evidence. Touches nothing on disk. Run
this first, always.
.TP
.B quarantine
Re\-runs the same scan and moves every \fBSAFE\fR candidate into
\fIquarantine_root\fR\fB/\fIYYYY\-MM\-DD\fR, recording each move \-
original path, quarantine path, class, reason, evidence, and a
content hash of every contained file \- in
\fIquarantine_root\fR\fB/manifest.json\fR. Nothing is quarantined
twice: re\-running after a prior quarantine simply finds fewer (or no)
candidates, since the originals are gone from their old paths.
.TP
.B verify
Non\-mutating. Re\-hashes every not\-yet\-purged manifest entry against
what is actually on disk (catches tampering or anything removed from
quarantine outside this tool) and flags entries older than
\fIpurge_after_days\fR as ready for a manual purge. Safe to run from
cron.
.TP
.B purge
The only step that deletes anything. For each manifest entry older
than \fIpurge_after_days\fR, re\-verifies its hash and deletes it only
if the hash still matches (a changed hash is skipped, not forced).
Refuses outright without \fB\-\-confirm\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-config \ \fIPATH\fR
Path to \fBsenechal.json\fR. Defaults to the repo's own
\fBsenechal.json\fR next to this script; in normal use this should
instead point at \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/senechal/senechal.json\fR (see
\fBREADME.md\fR, \(lqWhere the config lives\(rq).
.TP
.B \-\-confirm
Required by \fBpurge\fR. Without it, \fBpurge\fR prints a refusal and
exits \fB1\fR without deleting anything.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Every threshold is read from the \fBdeclutter\fR block of
\fBsenechal.json\fR, never hardcoded here \- see
\fBsenechal.json.example\fR for the full schema and its rationale.
.TP
.B quarantine_root
Where quarantined items and the manifest live. Default
\fB~/.senechal\-quarantine\fR.
.TP
.B purge_after_days
Grace period before \fBverify\fR flags an entry and \fBpurge\fR will
touch it. Default 30.
.TP
.B exclude
Paths never treated as candidates, checked before any pattern.
\fB.git\fR is additionally always skipped, hardcoded rather than
configurable, so a thin exclude list can't accidentally expose it.
.TP
.B regenerable.roots \fR/\fB regenerable.patterns
Where and what to whole\-tree\-match for the \fBregenerable\fR class.
.TP
.B stale_downloads.roots \fR/\fB .stale_days \fR/\fB .extensions
Where, how old, and which extensions for the \fBstale_download\fR
class.
.TP
.B debris.roots \fR/\fB .patterns \fR/\fB .treat_zero_byte_as_debris
Where and what to recursively match for the \fBdebris\fR class.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
\fBscan\fR or \fBquarantine\fR completed (including finding zero
candidates \- that is a real, correct answer); or \fBverify\fR found
the manifest fully consistent with nothing past its grace period; or
\fBpurge\fR ran (with or without anything actually past\-grace to
delete).
.TP
.B 1
\fBverify\fR found a manifest entry that is missing from disk or whose
content hash no longer matches what was recorded at quarantine time;
or \fBpurge\fR was invoked without \fB\-\-confirm\fR.
.TP
.B 2
Could not proceed at all: \fBsenechal.json\fR is unreadable, its
\fBdeclutter\fR block has no roots configured under any of the three
classes, or (on \fBverify\fR only) the quarantine directory does not
exist yet. Treat as \(lqcould not check\(rq, not a pass.
.TP
.B 3
\fBverify\fR found one or more manifest entries past
\fIpurge_after_days\fR. Nothing is broken; a manual \fBpurge\fR
decision is due.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I senechal.json
(or \fB$SENECHAL_CONFIG\fR / \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/senechal/senechal.json\fR) \-
the \fBdeclutter\fR block that drives every subcommand.
.TP
.I ~/.senechal-quarantine/manifest.json
The append\-only ledger of every quarantine and purge action, keyed by
original path and content hash.
.SH SAFETY MODEL
Per \fBREADME.md\fR's acting\-authority rules,
.B quarantine
is a reversible operational fix (a plain \fBmv\fR back from the
manifest's recorded path undoes it) and so is the one subcommand this
tool ever performs unattended.
.B purge
is not reversible and is never run except by hand, with
\fB\-\-confirm\fR, regardless of caller.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR garde (1)
\- off\-machine backup coverage, the recoverability gate's second leg.
.PP
\fBtools/home-declutter.py\fR's own module docstring, for the full
rationale behind the two\-gate design and the debris class. A separate,
auto\-derived man page for the bashified \fBdebarrasse\fR verb exists
in realisateur's verb\-build output; it documents a wrapper contract
(\fB\-\-force\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR, \fB\-\-json\fR, a \fBlist\fR
subcommand) that does not match the flags described here \-\- that
page describes the verb wrapper, this page describes the script it
wraps.
